#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110461 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8553da6-42ce-5b77-97c4-7d9e2ee55f85" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "ef4a57fd-8ade-5b7d-9d6e-0fabd228e603" :question/math-academy-id "e-3546" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{2} \\sin{x} \\cos{x} = -\\sin{x}$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi.$ If the solutions $x_1, x_2, x_3$ are written in ascending order, then what is $x_2\\cdot x_3?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0, \\qquad \\sqrt{2} \\cos{x} + 1 =0.\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{x} = 0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$  are $x = 0,\\, \\pi.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\sqrt{2} \\cos{x} + 1= 0,$ or equivalently $\\cos{x} =-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$ The solution to this equation for $0\\leq x \\leq \\pi$  is $x =\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0,\\quad x_2=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},\\quad x_3= \\pi.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nx_2\\cdot x_3 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\cdot  \\pi=\\dfrac{3\\pi^2}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110462 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11c93af6-aa99-5071-8946-885cda9cd8e7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation by Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "4c5e27aa-f9a5-54c0-a15c-14b923eec893" :question/math-academy-id "e-3548" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\sin{\\theta }\\tan{\\theta }-\\sin{\\theta } = 0$ for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta <  360^\\circ.$ If the solutions $\\theta_1, \\theta_2, \\theta_3, \\theta_4$ are written in ascending order, then what is the numerical value of $\\theta_2\\cdot \\theta_3?$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\tan{\\theta}$ is not defined at $\\theta = 90^\\circ , 270^\\circ,$ so these values cannot be solutions to the equation. \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\sin{\\theta } = 0, \\qquad \\sqrt{3}\\tan{\\theta } - 1 =0.\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{\\theta} = 0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta <  360^\\circ$  are $\\theta = 0^\\circ, \\, 180^\\circ.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\sqrt{3}\\tan{\\theta } - 1 =0,$ or equivalently $\\tan{\\theta} =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$ The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq \\theta <  360^\\circ$ are $\\theta=30^\\circ, \\, 210^\\circ.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = 0^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_2= 30^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_3=180^\\circ, \\quad \\theta_4= 210^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\theta_2\\cdot \\theta_3 =30 \\cdot  180 = 5\\,400.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110463 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0f24e0-da03-5fa3-b3dc-bb383068ae15" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing Reciprocal Functions by Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "362274e9-6980-5e3b-896b-db42cfc41a9e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3550" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\csc{x} \\tan{x} = \\tan{x}$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x <  360^\\circ.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\csc{x}$ is not defined at the points where $\\sin{x} = 0.$ On the interval $0^\\circ \\leq x <  360^\\circ,$ the excluded values are $x = 0^\\circ , 180^\\circ.$\n\nOn the other hand, the function $\\tan{x}$ is not defined at the points where $\\cos{x} = 0.$ In the given interval, the excluded values are $x = 90^\\circ , 270^\\circ.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\csc{x} - 1 = 0, \\qquad \\tan{x} = 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\csc{x} -1= 0,$ or equivalently, $\\csc x=1.$ The solution to this equation for $0 ^\\circ \\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ$ is $x = 90^\\circ.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\tan{x} = 0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ\\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ$  are $x = 0^\\circ,\\, 180^\\circ.$\n\nFinally, taking into account the values excluded from the interval, we conclude that the given equation has no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110464 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dea3f3b-5c9b-5005-996a-792a6902fcce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Some Coordinates on a Graph by Solving a Trigonometric Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "0aff48c8-380d-504b-a044-8ba08a2617a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3551" :question/problem "The graph shows the curve $y = 2\\sin{x}\\cos{x}$ and the curve $y = \\cos{x}$ on the interval $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ.$ The curves intersect at the points $A,$ $B,$ $C,$ and $D,$ as shown. Find $x$-coordinates of these points.\n\n![](images/d3/d3462ebb123dcaf59391787ffae5b39dea5809eefe656cfda2f17498259dc1b6.png)" :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives \nthe equation\n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\cos{x}  = 0, \\qquad 2\\sin{x} - 1 = 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\cos{x} = 0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$ are $x= 90^\\circ, \\, 270^\\circ.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\sin{x} - 1  = 0,$ or equivalently $\\sin{x}=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$ are $x = 30^\\circ,\\, 150^\\circ.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\nx_1=30^\\circ, \\quad x_2=90^\\circ,\\quad x_3=150^\\circ, \\quad x_4 = 270^\\circ.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11c93af6-aa99-5071-8946-885cda9cd8e7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32930bd5-ee8e-5a03-8ebb-b6b921877137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542775} {:answer/id #uuid "6a1ff96a-cf38-5ddb-9d6e-419a210d9af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542926} {:answer/id #uuid "80900602-f3a4-5944-ae40-68a14b46ef39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542984} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef7b925-46c5-59ff-8710-15af50538fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543062} {:answer/id #uuid "cb250ce7-63ea-5a3c-8019-6b5aba0c0efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32930bd5-ee8e-5a03-8ebb-b6b921877137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542775} :answer-field/id #uuid "337bac18-a16d-5942-8b9d-bc00008b3b4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a638344-d920-5adc-900d-8c273723b689" :question/math-academy-id "q-23987" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1)=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order, then what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "By the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solution to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘}$  is $x={90}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘}$ are $x={30}^{∘},{150}^{∘}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={30}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={90}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={150}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=30\\cdot 150=4\\,500.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11c93af6-aa99-5071-8946-885cda9cd8e7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13d379b4-fd86-5552-9424-edc289fe4370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542690} {:answer/id #uuid "3c5a0acb-466f-54ae-8690-a2b138fe3d18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542804} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0c1c93-dac8-5aff-a40b-4d6ff43c11c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542807} {:answer/id #uuid "46f53105-09bf-502b-b472-e933fae8750c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542832} {:answer/id #uuid "b029ed80-929b-58d1-85a6-c1480f689058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46f53105-09bf-502b-b472-e933fae8750c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542832} :answer-field/id #uuid "853b9a45-07a0-5d79-aac9-5ddaf7fb8d59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f95ce679-5df8-536b-9a6e-bc83e05e288c" :question/math-academy-id "q-23996" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2)=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "By the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x\\le {180}^{∘}$  are $x={0}^{∘},{180}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2.$ But since $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2$ has no solution.\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={0}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0\\cdot 180=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8553da6-42ce-5b77-97c4-7d9e2ee55f85" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6afda1d5-bf04-5849-acce-30e908b96c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107768} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef3adf3-04fa-5f75-a8bf-08d06229dccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542722} {:answer/id #uuid "3a612fb7-85ca-5e1e-a0d9-05385206ab45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542797} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fa603c-0bd7-5243-8435-dee5cbc797af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543122} {:answer/id #uuid "f842e257-c55e-5142-8502-c18e6a995408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a612fb7-85ca-5e1e-a0d9-05385206ab45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542797} :answer-field/id #uuid "039d55a3-f5ec-5e3a-b6e9-2733fee9f9fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e42167e1-42b5-5316-b2f2-add419b65958" :question/math-academy-id "q-24002" :question/problem "Consider the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, then what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $  are $x=-\\pi ,\\,0.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $  are $x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\pi ,\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-\\pi )\\cdot (-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11c93af6-aa99-5071-8946-885cda9cd8e7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2536465b-5ecd-5518-9be7-2990d421c9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542736} {:answer/id #uuid "3bfe7f99-5d4f-5ab8-8bd3-cc14bb9336f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542802} {:answer/id #uuid "5492dbb1-a688-58a2-8864-d6418a74cf80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542868} {:answer/id #uuid "783b0db3-0d5a-52d0-8afa-ca071f9c1bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542958} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4f4608-c3d1-5144-888e-f41391f17a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bfe7f99-5d4f-5ab8-8bd3-cc14bb9336f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542802} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfaff15c-8394-5bdb-8554-c3a83444ae51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c63aa2a-bac5-5697-a40d-14aec41163e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-24178" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$ \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$  are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+3=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={120}^{∘},\\,{300}^{∘}.$\n\nTaking into account that ${90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}$ are excluded values, and combining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={120}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={300}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=120\\cdot 300=36\\,000.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11c93af6-aa99-5071-8946-885cda9cd8e7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7e6317a-c800-5e65-832d-95b3f9a2062b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7e6317a-c800-5e65-832d-95b3f9a2062b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543084} :answer-field/id #uuid "5de29ce3-b3ef-56b1-91e3-39478ee6339a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543348} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4278b12b-558c-5213-b12e-e1f931274ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/iden