} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49fd94b7-d87e-5482-b74a-14a44acf4a47" :question/math-academy-id "q-337256" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}$\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-3}}$\\,{x}_{4}=$ \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=-\\pi ,\\,0.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\pi ,\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8553da6-42ce-5b77-97c4-7d9e2ee55f85" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "711256d5-4b36-5ac3-9517-2d23a59d4b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 