wer.type/math} :answer/value "225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8ac7c9a-5dac-540e-bb67-d19ed21fe95a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543121} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f021667-0727-5fa1-9c53-57fb0ae2a54b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543308} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e282431c-d64e-5706-b406-9225ec237285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e282431c-d64e-5706-b406-9225ec237285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543217} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bacd1dc-e01d-53e8-89a6-9fe722ce47a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc1ed235-5329-5107-b309-feec267a8cde" :question/math-academy-id "q-337369" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0,$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the functions $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ On the interval $0\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ the excluded values are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$ \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=0.$ Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ so it is never equal to zero. Therefore, this equation has no solutions.\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={45}^{∘},\\,{225}^{∘}.$\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={45}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={225}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11c93af6-aa99-5071-8946-885cda9cd8e7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caa5f594-8176-5c26-b7e8-5e8be95850cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caa5f594-8176-5c26-b7e8-5e8be95850cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543167} :answer-field/id #uuid "2005716b-94ca-58b9-b1e8-6bc420209440" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543309} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b04751f9-d84f-529e-9019-27752cc3a07a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b04751f9-d84f-529e-9019-27752cc3a07a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543099} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c6598f4-0fbf-50af-884c-720c85efe7f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81da0bad-95e8-53a0-9f19-ddc55b628c5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338464" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={120}^{∘},\\,{300}^{∘}.$\n\nTaking into account that ${90}^{∘}$ and ${270}^{∘}$ are excluded values, and arranging the remaining solutions in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={120}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={300}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dea3f3b-5c9b-5005-996a-792a6902fcce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Some Coordinates on a Graph by Solving a Trigonometric Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99dce7ce-64f2-5c0a-9837-190cd80030e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99dce7ce-64f2-5c0a-9837-190cd80030e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543043} :answer-field/id #uuid "6de9e1be-49f8-52e7-a91c-12166b2a5eb3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543360} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2224658-7330-566b-9cbb-e995ea4833aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2224658-7330-566b-9cbb-e995ea4833aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543216} :answer-field/id #uuid "7726603e-9dba-5342-9622-38dad57d93fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa82973f-2912-57c5-acdd-b4b8b9abbe44" :question/math-academy-id "q-338486" :question/problem "![](images/25/2593d513ce130090a84cc26e71bf321960712a6ccb2d8ff82e55e4728cc0657c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=2{cos}^{2}⁡x$ and the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the interval $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ The curves intersect at the points $A,B,C,$ and $D,$ as shown. Determine the exact values of the $x$-coordinates of points $A$ and $D,$ in radians.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of point $A$ is\n{{field-1}}\nand the $x$-coordinate of point $D$ is\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives the equation $2{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1=0.\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nCombining the solutions and ordering them from smallest to largest, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of point $A$ is\n$\\frac{\\pi }{3}$\nand the $x$-coordinate of point $D$ is\n$\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0f24e0-da03-5fa3-b3dc-bb383068ae15" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing Reciprocal Functions by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "376ae09a-f4d9-52aa-9a15-740441b9501e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "376ae09a-f4d9-52aa-9a15-740441b9501e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542785} :answer-field/id #uuid "a695e8c9-c3e6-555c-96b5-adf9863adba8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543410} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed0b24e5-bc5d-5d6d-81fb-99045cda4543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed0b24e5-bc5d-5d6d-81fb-99045cda4543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543242} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa17f357-450d-5ebf-a036-6faafdfbae46" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "68f4987d-ed26-5738-89d9-828f518f37d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-338519" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1=0,$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Note that the functions $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ are not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0$ or $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ On the interval $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ the excluded values are $x=0,\\,\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pm \\pi .$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)-(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1) & =0 \\\\ (2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1=0,\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-2.$ But since $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-2$ has no solution.\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0f24e0-da03-5fa3-b3dc-bb383068ae15" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing Reciprocal Functions by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aad9c1f-4c6f-563f-99c9-525eddb0b1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542663} {:answer/id #uuid "419afc3f-fae1-5b04-9a9b-aa8c1df5d0f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542817} {:answer/id #uuid "92843f39-23a0-5698-ac73-cdde16a2cae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543023} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe443fa-730f-540d-a625-fe81728241b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543145} {:answer/id #uuid "fe067070-e9db-5e0a-a3c7-2a529b04f2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92843f39-23a0-5698-ac73-cdde16a2cae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543023} :answer-field/id #uuid "7de40b82-9df6-5bea-a14d-15c9e7e67103" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf8eafa9-ba3d-54d7-ae84-18acaa32c81c" :question/math-academy-id "q-65411" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0$ for $0\\le x\\le \\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order, then what is ${x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ In the given interval, the excluded value is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for $0\\le x\\le \\pi $  are $x=0,\\,\\pi .$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2.$ The solution to this equation for $0\\le x\\le \\pi $ is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=0,\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{3}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\pi =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110685 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dea3f3b-5c9b-5005-996a-792a6902fcce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Some Coordinates on a Graph by Solving a Trigonometric Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d160033-4386-508d-b64f-d80f03cec15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542759} {:answer/id #uuid "60466ba4-ad46-5796-b614-863d5b0d8100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89\\,100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542900} {:answer/id #uuid "a0773ce3-1a02-5702-aa2a-8171a60d2410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543065} {:answer/id #uuid "d4460c25-6796-5ccf-9fcf-f9e71d0d8b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29\\,700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543186} {:answer/id #uuid "f5af0e2c-ca25-50e1-858f-d52c9e9818f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0773ce3-1a02-5702-aa2a-8171a60d2410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543065} :answer-field/id #uuid "82becc71-7589-5d67-812a-70d9948d739d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22a4714b-c9b9-595d-bd56-cdf122336677" :question/math-academy-id "q-82331" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1dd38a3d7e9969b9cc74c043261fd2f4d3ee1c1293a623fd812e31256b4ec766.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the curve $y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the interval ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘}.$ The curves intersect at the points $A,B,C$ and $D$, as shown. Find the product of the $x$-coordinates of $B$ and $D.$" :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$ values of both graphs are the same. This gives \nthe equation $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={210}^{∘},\\,{330}^{∘}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={90}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={210}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={270}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={330}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the product of the $x$-coordinates of $B$ and $D$ is \n\n$$\n210\\cdot 330=69\\,300.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0f24e0-da03-5fa3-b3dc-bb383068ae15" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing Reciprocal Functions by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a30a8d1-f851-5f5b-835f-f518b55fd72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542660} {:answer/id #uuid "7ede5552-7e21-5d3d-aff7-357e5c363c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542980} {:answer/id #uuid "860130af-c136-524a-bff5-324d7b734e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542996} {:answer/id #uuid "8f90525e-3c3f-5e84-a145-5febbe233060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543013} {:answer/id #uuid "ed071515-aa7c-5061-b71d-58691f3581bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed071515-aa7c-5061-b71d-58691f3581bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543241} :answer-field/id #uuid "391ba5d5-1569-5749-804d-be4aff1f7cf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a135a2c5-e84d-52d5-89de-19fd76614f68" :question/math-academy-id "q-90621" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1=0$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the function $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ On the interval $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ the excluded values are $x=-\\pi ,\\,0.$\n\nOn the other hand, the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ On the interval $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ the excluded values are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x(2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1)-(2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1) & =0 \\\\ (2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1)(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-2.$ But since $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2$ has no solution.\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nCombining all solutions from both equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}=-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$"}]}
